Below is a concise “tool‑kit” of the most **reliable, production‑ready** techniques that are used in today’s game‑engine tool‑chains (Unity, Unreal, custom C++/C# tools, Houdini‑style pipelines, etc.).  The methods are grouped by the problem they solve, and each entry lists:

* **What it does** – the core algorithmic idea.  
* **Typical implementation** – the library or language primitives you can call.  
* **Why it’s trusted in production** – performance, numerical stability, or community adoption.  
* **Key references** – links to the official docs, papers, or release notes (with the date the information was current).

---

## 1. 3‑D Coordinate Reasoning & Transforms  

| Technique | How it works | Common implementations | Production notes | References |
|-----------|--------------|------------------------|------------------|------------|
| **Column‑major 4×4 matrices** (model‑view‑projection) | Stores transformations as a single matrix; composition is matrix multiplication. | Unity `Matrix4x4`, Unreal `FMatrix`, GLM `glm::mat4` | Deterministic, matches GPU expectations, easy to serialize. Use **right‑handed** or **left‑handed** consistently; most engines default to left‑handed (e.g., Unity) or right‑handed (e.g., Unreal). | Unity Matrix docs, 2024‑06 <https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/Matrix4x4.html> |
| **Quaternions for rotation** | Unit quaternion `q = (x, y, z, w)` representing axis‑angle without gimbal lock. | Unity `Quaternion`, GLM `glm::quat`, DirectX Math `XMVECTORF3` + `XMQuaternion*` | Provides smooth interpolation (`Slerp`, `NLerp`) and stable composition (`q * p`). Store as `float4` for SIMD. | GLM Quat docs, 2024‑07 <https://glm.g-truc.net/0.9.9/api/a00148.html> |
| **Euler‑angle ↔ Quaternion conversion** | `ToQuaternion` using half‑angle formulas; `ToEuler` via `atan2` of quaternion components. | `glm::quat_cast`, Unity `Quaternion.Euler` | Use only for UI/asset import; never store as primary rotation. | OpenGL Math FAQ, 2023‑12 <https://glm.g-truc.net/0.9.2/faq.html> |
| **Transform hierarchy** | Local → world: `M = T_parent * M_local`. Inverse: `M_local = M_parent⁻¹ * M_world`. | Unity `Transform`, Unreal `USceneComponent` | Guarantees hierarchical correctness; avoid recomputing inverses every frame – cache `parent->world` and use it for child updates. | Unreal Transform System, 2024‑05 <https://docs.unrealengine.com/en-US/Programming/Thumbnails/TransformSystem/index.html> |
| **SIMD‑friendly structs** | Pack `float3`/`float4` into `__m128` (SSE) or `float32x4_t` (NEON). | DirectX Math `XMVECTOR`, GLM `glm::packed_vec3`, Unity `Vector3` (CPU fallback) | Enables batch matrix‑vector multiplies; most engines expose a `Mathf`/`FMath` API that auto‑vectorizes. | DirectX Math Guide, 2024‑08 <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/directx/math/architecture> |

**Best practice tip****Best practice tip:** Cache your parent–world matrix combination and recalculate child matrices only when the parent changes. Use a “dirty flag” (bitfield) rather than timestamp checks to cut down on unnecessary recompute on large hierarchies.

